cyl\",\"color:4 cyl\",\"color:4 cyl\",\"color:6 cyl\",\"color:6 cyl\",\"color:6 cyl\",\"color:8 cyl\",\"color:8 cyl\",[],[],[]]},\"geometry\":null,\"panels\":[{\"name\":\"panel-1-1\",\"px0\":65.03936767578125,\"py0\":50.0367431640625,\"px1\":488.971126205965,\"py1\":320.2939610118316,\"x\":{\"type\":\"discrete\",\"transform\":\"identity\",\"data_lo\":1,\"data_hi\":3,\"native_lo\":0.5,\"native_hi\":3.5,\"breaks\":[1,2,3],\"labels\":[\"3 gears\",\"4 gears\",\"5 gears\"],\"aes\":\"x\",\"levels\":[\"3 gears\",\"4 gears\",\"5 gears\"]},\"y\":{\"type\":\"continuous\",\"transform\":\"identity\",\"data_lo\":0,\"data_hi\":28.2,\"native_lo\":-1.41,\"native_hi\":29.61,\"breaks\":[0,10,20],\"labels\":[\"0\",\"10\",\"20\"],\"aes\":\"y\"}}],\"colorbar\":null,\"marks\":null,\"displayList\":null,\"above\":null,\"flagged\":null,\"interactions\":{\"selections\":[],\"conditions\":[],\"filters\":[],\"binds\":[],\"source\":{\"on\":\"click\",\"values\":false}},\"options\":{\"raster\":false,\"detailMarks\":3000,\"maxZoom\":100,\"navigator\":false,\"navigatorHeight\":null,\"toolbar\":true,\"hoverMode\":\"closest\",\"crosshair\":false,\"legendClick\":\"select\",\"tooltipDelay\":0,\"tooltipFollow\":true,\"tooltipSticky\":false,\"a11y\":true,\"alt\":null,\"selectMode\":\"multiple\",\"group\":null,\"crosstalk\":null,\"style\":[],\"export\":{\"filename\":\"plot\",\"scale\":1}},\"provenance\":{\"on\":\"click\",\"byId\":{\"layer-1-bar-g1\":[1,4,6],\"layer-1-bar-g2\":[2,5,7],\"layer-1-bar-g3\":[3,8]},\"fields\":[\"cyl\",\"gear\",\"mpg\",\"id\"]}},\"evals\":[],\"jsHooks\":[]}</script>\n```\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\nClicking a bar shows the rows it was computed from, in a small popover. Under the\nhood the widget answers from the compiled scene's provenance rather than from a\nlookup you had to build: each grob knows which input rows it came from. Under Shiny\nthe same gesture sets `input$<id>_source`, and in plain HTML it fires a bubbling\n`vellum:source` DOM event you can listen for. Pass `inspect_source(values = TRUE)`\nto ship the row *values* too, at the cost of a heavier payload.\n\nOne detail you only notice when it is missing: when a plot has too many keyed marks\nto ship as per-element SVG, the widget draws the scene once as a raster and runs\ninteractions off a compact index. That raster is now rendered at 2× and encoded in\nmemory, so it is sharp on a retina screen without touching disk. The bounding boxes\nand panel rectangles stay in device pixels, so nothing about picking or pan/zoom\nchanges. See\n[A big scatter that stays interactive](big-interactive.qmd) for that path at\n120,000 points.\n",
